      "content": {
        "type": "quiz",
        "questions": [
          {
            "id": "q1",
            "type": "single",
            "question": "Which of the following items is NOT typically part of a smoke‑test checklist for OpenMAIC storage deployment?",
            "options": [
              {
                "value": "A",
                "label": "Verify that the storage service starts without errors"
              },
              {
                "value": "B",
                "label": "Confirm that data can be written and read back correctly"
              },
              {
                "value": "C",
                "label": "Run a full performance benchmark suite"
              },
              {
                "value": "D",
                "label": "Check that configuration files are present and syntactically correct"
              }
            ],
            "answer": [
              "C"
            ],
            "analysis": "Running a full performance benchmark suite is beyond the scope of a smoke test, which focuses on basic functionality. The other items (service start, read/write verification, config validation) are common checklist items.",
            "points": 10,
            "hasAnswer": true
          },
          {
            "id": "q2",
            "type": "multiple",
            "question": "Given the sample test result below, select all statements that are true.\n\nSample Result:\n- Service status: RUNNING\n- Write test: PASS\n- Read test: PASS\n- Latency: 120 ms (within acceptable range)\n- Disk usage: 45% of capacity\n\nSelect all that apply:",
            "options": [
              {
                "value": "A",
                "label": "The storage service is operational."
              },
              {
                "value": "B",
                "label": "The write functionality is failing."
              },
              {
                "value": "C",
                "label": "Latency meets the defined threshold."
              },
              {
                "value": "D",
                "label": "Disk space is critically low."
              }
            ],
            "answer": [
              "A",
              "C"
            ],
            "analysis": "A is correct because the service status is RUNNING. C is correct because latency of 120 ms is reported as within the acceptable range. B is false because the write test passed. D is false because 45% usage is not critical.",
            "points": 15,
            "hasAnswer": true
          }
        ]
      },
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "action_fcCkdK1h",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Alright, everyone, let's check what we've just covered about smoke‑test checklists for OpenMAIC storage deployments. Remember, a smoke test is a quick, high‑level verification that the most critical functions are working before we move on to deeper testing."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_wfhTBj69",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "First, think about the items that belong on that checklist: verifying the service starts, confirming you can write and read data, and making sure configuration files are present and syntactically correct. Those are all essential, lightweight checks. A full performance benchmark, however, is far more involved and belongs in a later, dedicated performance test phase, not in a smoke test."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_QwuK-ZiZ",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Now, look at the sample test result. The service status is RUNNING, both write and read tests passed, latency is 120 ms which is within the acceptable range, and disk usage is at 45 %—well below any critical threshold. From that we can conclude the storage service is operational, the write functionality is fine, latency meets the defined threshold, and there’s no immediate concern about disk space."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_EmFfZDt-",
          "type": "discussion",
          "topic": "Why is it important to keep smoke tests lightweight, and how does interpreting a quick test result help us catch issues early?",
          "prompt": "Share your thoughts on balancing thoroughness with speed in early testing stages.",
          "agentId": "default-4"
        }
      ],
